body:
Fixes #257.

## What

Guard the init loop in `binaryMultiMMCPredictionEstimate` (`cpp/non_iid/multi_mmc_test.h`) with `d+1 < L`, so `S[d+1]` is never read past the sample buffer.

```diff
   // initialize MMC counts
-  for(d=0; d<D_MMC; d++) {
+  for(d=0; (d<D_MMC) && (d+1 < L); d++) {
      curPattern = ((curPattern << 1) | (S[d]&1));
      (BINARYDICTLOC(d+1, curPattern))[S[d+1]&1] = 1;
      dictElems[d] = 1;
   }
```

## Why

`D_MMC == 16`, and the loop reads `S[d+1]` for `d` up to `D_MMC-1 = 15`, i.e. `S[16]`, guarded only by `assert(L>3)`. For any `4 <= L <= 16` this reads one byte past the end of the buffer allocated in `read_file_subset`. It is reachable from an untrusted file with the default command line. On a normal assert-enabled build, the over-read is silent inside MultiMMC, but execution later aborts in `binaryLZ78YPredictionEstimate`, so no overall assessment is emitted. AddressSanitizer exposes the underlying heap-buffer-overflow directly at `multi_mmc_test.h:38`. See #257 for the full reproduction.

The generic (non-binary) `multiMMCPredictionEstimate` already guards the identical loop with `if(d < N)` (`N = len-2`); this brings the binary path in line.

## What does not change

For `L > 16` the guard `d+1 < L` is always true, so no in-bounds run changes:

- AddressSanitizer clean on `L` in `{4,5,8,12,16}` (was: heap-buffer-overflow on each).
- `cpp/selftest/selftest` vs `refdata`: max delta `3.7e-10`, none above `1e-9` (unchanged platform deltas).
- Identical `Assessed min entropy` to the unpatched binary on `bin/truerand_8bit.bin` (`7.2338614551796505`).

Built and tested at the current `master` baseline on macOS (Apple clang + libomp).

